Identify the key historical figures associated with the Back to Africa movement and their influence on Rastafarianism
The key historical figures associated with the Back to Africa movement include Thomas Buchanan, Thomas Jefferson, James Monroe, Abraham Lincoln, James Madison, Daniel Webster, John Marshall, Francis Scott Key, and Paul Cuffee. These individuals were members of the American Colonization Society, which migrated over 13,000 blacks back to Africa. 

Paul Cuffee was a notable figure who explored the idea of black people returning to their native land due to limited opportunities for advancement in America. His ideas contributed to the broader Back-to-Africa movement.

The Back-to-Africa movement also influenced Rastafarianism, particularly through the efforts of Marcus Garvey. Garvey promoted the idea of returning to Africa and was associated with the early Rastafari movement. His influence is evident in the Afrocentric ideology of Rastafarianism, which emerged as a reaction against British colonial culture in Jamaica. Additionally, the crowning of Haile Selassie I of Ethiopia in 1930 played a significant role in shaping Rastafarian beliefs, as it was seen as fulfilling a Biblical prophecy by Christian clergymen like Leonard Howell.
