Identify the specific form of the death penalty that was abolished during Francois Mitterrand's presidency, including the legal context and timeline of its abolition.
The specific form of the death penalty abolished during François Mitterrand's presidency was the judicial use of the death penalty. On 9 October 1981, President François Mitterrand signed a law that prohibited the judicial system from using the death penalty and commuted the sentences of the six people on death row. This marked the formal abolition of the death penalty in France, which was later enshrined in the Constitution of the French Republic by Article 66-1, voted as a constitutional amendment on 19 February 2007. The death penalty had been declared illegal in 1981, and the guillotine, which had been the primary method of execution since the French Revolution, was no longer used.
