Identify the historical context and establishment of the State of Israel, including key events and dates leading to its founding.
The State of Israel was established on May 14, 1948, when David Ben-Gurion proclaimed the creation of a Jewish state in Eretz Israel. This event followed the withdrawal of the last British soldiers from Palestine, marking the end of the British Mandate. The establishment of Israel was driven by the desire to create a Jewish homeland, which had been a long-standing aspiration in Jewish history. Key events leading up to its founding include the mass expulsions and exoduses of Jews throughout history, which prompted the search for a refuge. In the 19th century, Jewish nationalism (Zionism) began to take shape, with Jews predominantly from Europe emigrating to Ottoman Palestine with the goal of establishing a new Jewish homeland. This movement culminated in the declaration of the State of Israel in 1948.
