Explain the definition and working mechanism of a party line in telephone terms, including its historical context and how it differed from other telephone connections.
The term "party line" in the context of telephone connections refers to a type of communication line that was used in the past, particularly in rural areas, where multiple households shared a single telephone line. This line was called a "party line" because it served multiple parties (households). Each household had a unique dial tone or signal to identify their connection, allowing them to communicate without interference.

Historically, party lines were common before the widespread availability of private telephone lines. They were often managed by a local operator or a designated individual who would switch between the connected parties. The working mechanism involved a switchboard or a simple electrical system that allowed the line to be shared among several users.

This concept of a "party line" in telephone terms differs from other telephone connections, such as private lines or modern digital networks, which are dedicated to a single user and do not involve sharing the same physical line among multiple parties.
