The historical context of the U.S. diplomatic presence in Tehran involved the Embassy of the United States, which was established in 1948 and designed by architect Ides van der Gracht. The embassy served as the U.S. diplomatic mission in Iran during the Imperial State of Iran. However, direct bilateral diplomatic relations between the U.S. and Iran were severed following the Iranian Revolution in 1979. This led to the seizure of the U.S. Embassy in Tehran on November 4, 1979, by a group of Iranian college students affiliated with the Muslim Student Followers of the Imam's Line, who supported the Iranian Revolution. The crisis, which lasted 444 days until January 20, 1981, marked the longest hostage crisis in recorded history. During this period, American President Jimmy Carter was in office, and he referred to the hostage-taking as an "entanglement" of "vengeance and mutual incomprehension."
