The Arabian Nights tales are traditionally attributed to a collection of folk tales compiled during the Islamic Golden Age. The compilation took form in the 10th century and reached its final form by the 14th century. These tales were gathered over many centuries by various authors, translators, and scholars across West, Central, and South Asia and North Africa. The stories have roots in ancient and medieval Arabic, Persian, Greek, Indian, Jewish, and Turkish folklore and literature. The work is often known in English as "The Arabian Nights," a name invented by early Western translators, though it originally had different titles in Arabic and Persian.
