Identify the historical context of Henry VIII's conflict with the Catholic Church and the establishment of the Church of England.
Henry VIII's conflict with the Catholic Church began when Pope Clement VII refused to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon, which Henry sought to obtain in order to marry Anne Boleyn. This refusal, coupled with Henry's desire for political control over the English church, led him to break away from the Roman Catholic Church. In 1534, he passed the Act of Supremacy, declaring himself the "Supreme Head of the Church of England," thereby establishing the Church of England and removing papal authority from English religious affairs. This marked the beginning of the English Reformation and the development of Anglicanism.
