Identify the toxic compound present in rhubarb leaves and explain its chemical structure and properties
The toxic compound present in rhubarb leaves is oxalic acid. Oxalic acid is a dicarboxylic acid with the chemical formula $ \text{C}_2\text{H}_2\text{O}_4 $. It is known for its ability to form insoluble salts with various cations, such as calcium, leading to the formation of calcium oxalate crystals. These crystals can cause health risks, including irritation and damage to the mouth, throat, and digestive tract. Oxalic acid is also nephrotoxic, meaning it can be harmful to the kidneys, and may contribute to the development of kidney stones. The toxicity of oxalic acid is due to its ability to bind with metal ions, leading to the formation of insoluble complexes that can interfere with normal physiological functions.
