Identify the anatomical location and developmental pathway of Graafian follicles from their origin in the ovary.
Graafian follicles originate in the ovary as primary follicles, which contain oocytes established before birth. These oocytes remain dormant until they are stimulated to mature during puberty. The development of Graafian follicles involves a process called folliculogenesis, progressing from primordial to preovulatory stages. During this process, the follicle matures and eventually ruptures at the stigma, an area on the ovarian surface where the follicle bursts to release the secondary oocyte during ovulation. This rupture allows the secondary oocyte to be captured by the fallopian tube.
