Identify the classification and defining characteristics of basalt as a type of igneous rock
Basalt is a fine-grained or glassy igneous rock primarily composed of plagioclase feldspar and clinopyroxene (augite). It is defined by its mineral composition, which includes at least 65% feldspar in the form of plagioclase, and it is classified under the International Union of Geological Sciences (IUGS) scheme. Basalt is the most common volcanic rock type on Earth and is a key component of oceanic crust. It has a high structural integrity due to its cooled lava origin, distinguishing it from sedimentary rocks like tuff. The average density of basalt is 3.0 g/cm³.
