Explain the geological conditions and transformation mechanisms that lead to the formation of marble from its parent rock.
Marble forms from the metamorphism of sedimentary carbonate rocks, most commonly limestone or dolomite. This process occurs when these rocks are subjected to high heat and pressure, typically due to tectonic forces deep within the Earth's crust. The intense heat and pressure cause the original carbonate minerals, such as calcite in limestone, to recrystallize into a denser, interlocking mosaic of carbonate crystals. This transformation alters the original texture and structure of the parent rock, resulting in the formation of marble. The purity of the marble depends on the composition of the original limestone; pure white marble is formed from very pure, silicate-poor limestone.
