Identify the term used to describe large accumulations of algae in polluted water bodies and explain their ecological impact
The term used to describe large accumulations of algae in polluted water bodies is "algal bloom." Algal blooms are characterized by a rapid increase in the population of algae, which can lead to discoloration of the water due to their pigments. These blooms can be harmful, referred to as "harmful algal blooms" (HAB), and can have significant ecological impacts. They can lead to fish die-offs, cities cutting off water to residents, or states closing fisheries. Additionally, certain types of algae are toxic, and their overgrowth can result in the toxins being passed up the food chain, affecting edibles like clams and ultimately impacting seabirds, marine mammals, and humans, potentially causing illness or death.
