Analyze the environmental conditions that promote the formation of these algae growths and their effects on aquatic ecosystems
Environmental conditions that promote the formation of algae growths include warm temperatures, shallow water, and high-nutrient conditions. Nutrients enter aquatic environments through surface runoff from agricultural pollution, urban runoff from fertilized lawns and other landscaped properties, and sewage treatment plants lacking nutrient control systems. Atmospheric pollution also contributes to nutrient input.

The effects of these algae growths on aquatic ecosystems can be significant. Algal blooms can lead to oxygen depletion in the water, which can harm or kill fish and other aquatic life. Additionally, some algal blooms produce toxins that are harmful to both aquatic organisms and humans. These blooms can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem by altering water quality and affecting the food chain.
