Explain the chemical structure of CFCs and identify the specific reactive component responsible for ozone depletion
CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons) are organic compounds composed of carbon, chlorine, and fluorine atoms. Their chemical structure typically consists of a central carbon atom bonded to multiple chlorine and fluorine atoms. For example, a common CFC is chlorodifluoromethane (CF₂Cl₂), which has a single carbon atom surrounded by two fluorine atoms, one chlorine atom, and two hydrogen atoms.

The specific reactive component responsible for ozone depletion is the chlorine atom. When CFCs reach the stratosphere, they are dissociated by ultraviolet light, releasing chlorine atoms. These chlorine atoms act as catalysts in the ozone depletion process, breaking down ozone molecules (O₃) into oxygen molecules (O₂). Each chlorine atom can catalyze the breakdown of thousands of ozone molecules before being removed from the stratosphere.
