Explain the astrophysical process by which a cloud of gas and dust contracts to form a star, including the role of gravity, temperature, and nuclear fusion.
The astrophysical process by which a cloud of gas and dust contracts to form a star begins with a dense region within a molecular cloud, primarily composed of hydrogen and helium. Gravity plays a crucial role in this process, as it pulls the gas and dust together, causing the cloud to collapse under its own weight. As the cloud collapses, conservation of angular momentum leads to the formation of a rotating disk around the central region.

The gravitational potential energy is converted into thermal energy, increasing the temperature and pressure at the core of the collapsing cloud. This increased temperature and pressure eventually lead to the formation of a protostar. As the protostar continues to accumulate mass and heat, the core becomes sufficiently dense and hot to initiate nuclear fusion, where hydrogen atoms fuse to form helium, releasing vast amounts of energy. This marks the birth of a main-sequence star.
