Explain the anatomical structure of slugs, focusing on their respiratory and sensory systems to determine the presence or absence of a nose
Slugs do not have a nose in the traditional sense, as they lack a nostril or olfactory organ. Their respiratory system is primarily characterized by the pneumostome, a small opening on the right side of the mantle that serves as their breathing hole. This structure allows for gas exchange, similar to a gill in some aquatic mollusks.

Regarding their sensory systems, slugs rely on other structures for sensing their environment. They possess chemoreceptors and tactile sensors distributed across their body, particularly around the head and foot, which help them detect chemicals and physical stimuli. These sensory organs enable them to navigate and respond to their surroundings without the need for a nose.
