Identify the historical and symbolic origins of barber shop poles, including the significance of their striped design.
The historical origins of barber shop poles date back to the Middle Ages. Originally, these poles were used by barber-surgeons, who performed a variety of medical procedures such as tooth extraction, cupping, leeching, bloodletting, enemas, and even amputations. The pole served as a visual sign to indicate the presence of a barber's shop.

The symbolic significance of the striped design is rooted in its resemblance to a helix, which was believed to represent the spiral of the human body or the winding path of veins. This imagery was thought to symbolize the barber's ability to work with the body's natural structures. The colors of the stripes—typically red, white, and blue in the United States, and red and white in many other countries—may have been chosen for their associations with medical practices and cleanliness. Over time, the barber's pole evolved into a more recognizable symbol of a place where haircuts and shaves are provided, though it has also sparked some controversy in the hairstyling industry.
